---
globs: **/*.cjs, **/*.js, **/*.jsx, **/*.mjs, **/*.ts, **/*.tsx, **/*.vue
alwaysApply: false
---

Dependency rule:
- Layers, from inner to outer: entities, use cases, adapters, frameworks and UI
- Source code dependencies point inward only: a layer may import from inner layers, never from outer ones
- Use cases and entities never import framework or UI code, and never perform I/O directly (e.g. browser APIs, HTTP, storage)

Layers:
- Entities: core business types and rules, the code least likely to change when the UI or storage changes; no I/O
- Use cases: one use case per application action (e.g. `cancelSubscription`, `publishPost`, `sendMessage`); they orchestrate entities and reach external systems only through ports
- Adapters: wrap external systems (e.g. browser APIs, HTTP, storage) and convert data between external formats and internal formats; when a use case declares a port, the adapter implements it
- Frameworks and UI: rendering, user input, and glue code that wires adapters to use cases; no business logic

Boundaries:
- A port is an interface that a use case declares for an external system it needs
- Pass adapters to use cases as function arguments; do not add a dependency injection library
- Let only entities and simple data structures cross boundaries, never database rows or framework objects (e.g. DOM events, HTTP responses, router objects)

Scope:
- Create use cases and ports when a feature has business logic (e.g. calculations, decisions, validation)
- When a feature only fetches and displays data, the UI may call an adapter directly
